1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,020
Let's take a look at why we might prefer to use a reflection workflow rather than just prompting an LLM once and having it directly generate the answer and be done with it.

2
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,600
With direct generation, you just prompt the LLM with an instruction and let it generate an answer.

3
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:19,820
So you will ask an LLM to write an essay about black holes and have it just generate the text,

4
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:25,800
or have it write the Python functions to calculate compound interest and have it just write the code directly.

5
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:31,100
The prompt examples you see here are also called zero-shot prompting.

6
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:33,160
Let me explain what zero-shot means.

7
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:41,860
In contrast to zero-shot prompting, a related approach is to include one or more examples of what you want the output to look like in your prompt.

8
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:48,160
And this is known as one-shot prompting if in the prompt you include one example of a desired input-output pair,

9
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:53,160
or two-shot or few-shot prompting depending on how many such examples you include in your prompt.

10
00:00:53,160 --> 00:01:00,820
And so zero shot prompting refers to, if you include zero examples, if you don't include any examples, of the desired outputs that you want.

11
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:15,140
But don't worry if you aren't yet familiar with these terms, the important thing is that in the examples you see here, you're just prompting the arm to directly generate an answer in one go, which I'm also calling zero shot prompting because we include zero examples.

12
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:22,960
It turns out that multiple studies have shown that reflection improves on the performance of direct generation on a variety of tasks.

13
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:35,780
This diagram is adapted from the research paper by Medan and others, and this shows a range of different tasks being implemented with different models and with and without reflection.

14
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:49,660
The way to read this diagram is to look at these pairs of adjacent light followed by dark colored bars, where the light bar shows zero shot prompting and the dark bar shows the same model but with reflection.

15
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:57,260
And the colors blue, green, and red show experiments run with different models, such as GPT 3.5 and GPT 4.

16
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:06,220
And what you see is, for many different applications, the dark bar that is with reflection is quite a bit higher than the light bar.

17
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:09,960
But of course, your knowledge may vary depending on your specific application.

18
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:13,960
Here are some more examples where reflection might be helpful.

19
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:22,180
If you are generating structured data, such as an HTML table, sometimes it may have incorrect formatting of the output.

20
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:26,000
So, a reflection prompt to validate the HTML code could be helpful.

21
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:40,120
If it's basic HTML, this may not help that much, since algorithms are pretty good at basic HTML, but especially if you have more complex structured outputs, like maybe a JSON data structure with a lot of nesting, then reflection may be more likely to spot bugs.

22
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:49,540
Or if you ask an LM to generate a sequence of steps to comprise a set of instructions to do something, such as how to brew a perfect cup of tea, sometimes the LM may miss steps.

23
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:54,920
And a reflection prompt to ask them to check instructions for coherence and completeness might help spot errors.

24
00:02:55,280 --> 00:03:04,380
Or something that I've actually worked on was using an LM to generate domain names, but sometimes the names it generates has an unintended meaning or may be really hard to pronounce.

25
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:13,180
And so, I've used reflection prompts to double check if the domain name has any problematic connotations or problematic meanings, or if the name is hard to pronounce.

26
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:20,420
And we actually use this at one of my teams, AI Fund, to help brainstorm domain names for startups that we're working on.

27
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:23,240
I want to show you a couple of examples of reflection prompts.

28
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:30,680
For brainstorming domain names, you might ask it to review the domain names you suggested, and then ask them to check if each name is easier to pronounce.

29
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:38,660
Check if each name might mean something negative in English or other languages, and then output a short list if only the names that satisfy these criteria.

30
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:47,660
Or to improve an email, you can write a reflection prompt to tell it to review the email first draft, check the tone, verify all facts, dates, and promises are accurate.

31
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:54,940
This would make sense in the context of the LM having been fed a number of facts and dates and so on in order to write the email drafts.

32
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:57,800
All this would be provided as part of the LM context.

33
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:01,960
And then based on any problems you may find, write the next draft of the email.

34
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:04,180
So some tips for writing reflection prompts.

35
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:11,640
It helps to clearly indicate that you wanted to review or to reflect on the first draft of the output.

36
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:16,400
And if you can specify a clear set of criteria, such as whether the domain name is easily pronounced,

37
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:18,260
and whether it may have negative connotations.

38
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:20,980
Or for email, check the tone and verify the facts.

39
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:26,940
Then that guides the LM better in reflecting and critiquing on the criteria that you care the most about.

40
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:35,400
I found that one of the ways I've learned to write better prompts is to read a lot of other prompts that other people have written.

41
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:43,860
Sometimes I'll actually download open source software and go and find the prompts in a piece of software that I think is especially well done.

42
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:46,540
To just go and read the prompts that the authors have written.

43
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:51,440
So with that, I hope you have a sense of how to write a basic reflection prompt,

44
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:56,440
and that maybe you even try it out in your own work to see if it helps give you better performance.

45
00:04:57,340 --> 00:05:04,600
In the next video, I'd like to share with you a fun example where we'll start to look at multimodal inputs and outputs,

46
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:09,620
where have an algorithm reflect on an image being generated, or a chart being generated.

47
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:11,220
Let's go take a look.

48
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:12,240
Quando we got started again, over at the last time we found a very powerful trend that we received a hint about.

